Title: "Set Theory: Axioms"
Id: "4b9a150c-46bd-49da-83b4-b106f402c3a4"


Text: "The entirety of classical mathematics can be systematically constructed
       from a single primitive notion—the *set*—and a single primitive binary
       relation—*membership*, denoted by $\in$. Numbers, algebraic structures,
       geometric spaces, and continuous functions are not independent
       ontological primitives; rather, each is formally realized as a set
       equipped with relations that are themselves collections of ordered pairs.
       This chapter establishes the axiomatic foundation of this universe, known
       as Zermelo--Fraenkel set theory (ZF), and introduces the fundamental
       operations that govern set formation."
Id: "a5b9d42b-f22f-4fd4-a867-29de537158c3"


Text: "In formal axiomatic set theory, a set is taken as an undefined primitive.
       We do not define what a set 'is' in terms of simpler components; instead,
       its nature is entirely characterized by the membership relation: for any
       objects $x$ and $X$, either $x \in X$ or $x \notin X$. Under the
       principle of *pure* set theory adopted here, there are no 'atoms' or
       'urelements' (entities with no members that are not themselves sets);
       every element of a set is itself a set. The declaration below formalizes
       the core structural commitments that define a set."
Id: "cbc6feb5-4f5e-41ef-bf59-342c981aa217"


[\set]
Declares: X
satisfies:
. \axiom.of.empty.set
. \axiom.of.infinity
. \axiom.of.union#given{X}
. \axiom.of.power.set#given{X}
. forAll: Y is \set
  then:
  . \axiom.of.extensionality#given{X := X; Y := Y}
  . \axiom.of.pairing#given{X := X; Y := Y}
Requires:
. capability: x_ "in" X :-> \\abstract
Enables:
. from: Y ::= {y__ : ...}
  capability: x_ "in" X :<->: x_ member_of Y
Documented:
. called: "set"
. description: "A set is the sole primitive object of the theory. The only
                question one may put to a set is which sets are its members, and
                two sets with the same members are the same set. Every other
                object built in this collection is ultimately a set."
Id: "a0759217-e1f6-412c-982a-0038cd17a3a1"


Text: "We write $x \notin X$ as standard shorthand for the negation
       $\neg (x \in X)$. The non-membership relation asserts that $x$ does not
       belong to the collection $X$."
Id: "48b22b9f-2acc-4e80-9148-667489d48ee1"


[x \.not.in./ X]
States:
when:
. x is \\anything
. X is \set
that:
. not: x "in"? X
Documented:
. written: "x? \notin X?"
. description: "The negation of membership: $x \notin X$ asserts that the set
                $x$ is not an element of the set $X$. It is shorthand for the
                statement that $x \in X$ fails to hold."
Id: "756e0f48-1c0e-4e05-b97a-9c4fcd577603"


SectionTitle: "The Empty Set"
Id: "8dc697a3-2ef0-4af9-8852-a994bdd8882d"


Text: "To construct a universe of sets, the deductive machinery must possess an
       initial seed—an object upon which subsequent operations can act. The
       simplest imaginable set is the one having no members whatsoever: the
       *empty set*, denoted $\emptyset$. The Axiom of the Empty Set guarantees
       that such a set exists, while the Axiom of Extensionality ensures that it
       is unique. In the cumulative hierarchy of sets, the empty set serves as
       the absolute base layer $V_0 = \emptyset$ from which all higher sets are
       iteratively generated."
Id: "7a54c26a-ea2e-4e38-ac69-3426f651c4d4"


[\empty.set]
Defines: X is \set
expresses:
. not:
  . exists: Y is \\anything
    suchThat: Y "in" X
Documented:
. called: "empty set"
. written: "\emptyset"
. description: "The empty set $\emptyset$ is the unique set having no members
                whatsoever. It is the seed from which the entire universe of
                sets -- and eventually the natural number $0$ -- is grown."
Id: "96908ddf-5fc5-455d-9cfd-eaf0fd6ada38"


Text: "A set is termed *nonempty* if it contains at least one element. In
       mathematical analysis and abstract algebra, nonemptiness is often an
       indispensable hypothesis: choice functions cannot select from empty sets,
       maximal elements require nonempty partially ordered sets, and sub-
       structures must contain identity elements."
Id: "6a19c255-0426-440b-a2e3-c97b1fe06bc4"


[\(nonempty)::set]
Refines: X
satisfies: X != \empty.set
Documented:
. adjective: "nonempty"
Id: "616950f0-7d70-48ff-a5ac-9d5d49272150"


SectionTitle: "The Core Axioms"
Id: "f2e995e5-7f69-49c8-90bc-46d8f77811f1"


Text: "We now present the core axiomatic postulates of Zermelo--Fraenkel set
       theory. The first postulate asserts the baseline existence of a
       memberless set, preventing the theory from describing an empty universe."
Id: "287b3b2f-e440-43b5-98be-b7310daf6aaf"


[\axiom.of.empty.set]
Axiom:
then:
. exists: X is \set
  suchThat:
  . forAll: Z is \set
    then:
    . not: Z "in"? X
Documented:
. called: "Axiom of Empty Set"
. description: "There is a set having no members: for every set $Z$, $Z \notin
                X$. Extensionality makes this set unique, so any construction
                with no members denotes the same empty set."
Id: "f2180719-568d-4084-982b-a71c5f195bf6"


Text: "The Axiom of Extensionality articulates the fundamental identity
       criterion of set theory: a set is completely and exhaustively determined
       by its members. Extensionality contrasts sharply with *intensional*
       descriptions: two descriptions that appear entirely distinct (such as
       'the set of even prime numbers' and 'the positive square root of $4$ in
       singleton form') represent the identical set $\{2\}$. Sets possess no
       internal ordering, no spatial arrangement, and no multiplicity of
       membership."
Id: "7f76409d-9233-44bf-984a-ede2358dcee5"


[\axiom.of.extensionality]
Axiom:
given: X, Y is \set
where:
. forAll: Z is \set
  then: (.Z "in"? X.) \.iff./ (.Z "in"? Y.)
then: X = Y
Documented:
. called: "Axiom of Extensionality"
. description: "Two sets are equal if and only if they have exactly the same
                elements. The identity of a set is therefore nothing more than
                the collection of its members, so any two constructions that
                yield the same elements yield the same set."
Id: "6fc3a89f-f8ef-420e-b1b6-6e5ed4905611"


Text: "The Axiom of Pairing provides the basic mechanism for assembling distinct
       entities into a higher-order collection. Given any two sets $X$ and $Y$,
       pairing asserts the existence of the unordered pair $\{X, Y\}$. When
       specialized to $X = Y$, it yields the singleton set $\{X\}$, allowing any
       individual set to be treated as an element of a new set."
Id: "7b45fe82-a42a-4591-9453-350baec1df28"


[\axiom.of.pairing]
Axiom:
given: X, Y is \set
then:
. exists: U is \set
  suchThat:
  . forAll: Z is \set
    then: (.Z "in"? U.) \.iff./ ((.Z = X.) \.or./ (.Z = Y.))
Documented:
. called: "Axiom of Pairing"
. description: "For any two sets $X$ and $Y$ there is a set $U$ whose members
                are exactly $X$ and $Y$. Applying it with $X = Y$ produces the
                set whose only member is $X$."
Id: "3219aca1-fba1-42a6-9a1f-209aa430c677"


Text: "While pairing constructs sets of size two, the Axiom of Union enables the
       consolidation of arbitrarily many sets. Given a set $X$ whose elements
       are themselves sets, the union $\bigcup X$ collects every element
       belonging to at least one member of $X$. In effect, the union axiom
       flattens nested set structures by one level of containment."
Id: "c0f76644-e25f-4e0c-8cf0-6daf86ad59df"


[\axiom.of.union]
Axiom:
given: X is \set
then:
. exists: U is \set
  suchThat:
  . forAll: Z is \set
    then:
    . have: Z "in" U
      iff:
      . exists: W is \set
        suchThat:
        . W "in"? X
        . Z "in"? W
Documented:
. called: "Axiom of Union"
. description: "For a set $X$ whose members are themselves sets, there is a set
                $U$ containing exactly those $Z$ that belong to some member $W$
                of $X$. It flattens one level of nesting, combining the elements
                of all the members of $X$."
Id: "60bcea09-6bf1-4917-b90f-64ceeb941558"


Text: "The operations of pairing and union allow us to assemble finite
       collections from existing elements, but they cannot escape the finite
       realm if all starting sets are finite. The Axiom of the Power Set
       provides an explosive leap in set-theoretic size: it asserts that all
       possible subcollections of a set $A$ can be gathered into a new set
       $\mathcal{P}(A)$. As Cantor's theorem will demonstrate, the power set of
       any set is strictly greater in cardinality than the set itself."
Id: "139b6eca-d1e5-4fae-97a4-1b05d96726f1"


[\axiom.of.power.set]
Axiom:
given: A is \set
then:
. exists: P is \set
  suchThat:
  . forAll: x is \set
    then: (.x "in"? P.) \.iff./ (.x \:subset?:/ A.)
Documented:
. called: "Axiom of Power Set"
. description: "For every set $A$ there is a set $P$ whose members are exactly
                the subsets of $A$. This power set collects together all of the
                ways of selecting elements from $A$."
Id: "8557cdb3-e02f-4b75-aed1-3dfd1cdee895"


Text: "The axioms introduced thus far cannot prove the existence of any infinite
       set. The Axiom of Infinity bridges the chasm between the finite and the
       transfinite by postulating a set $I$ containing the empty set $\emptyset$
       and closed under the von Neumann successor operation
       $S(Y) = Y \cup \{Y\}$. This inductive structure provides the foundational
       representation of the natural numbers $\mathbb{N}$."
Id: "9196ed0a-391f-4fb5-af13-ac8c65db0ae9"


[\axiom.of.infinity]
Axiom:
then:
. exists: I is \set
  suchThat:
  . allOf:
    . \empty.set "in"? I
    . forAll: Y is \set
      then: (.Y "in"? I.) \.implies./ (.\set.successor:of{Y} "in"? I.)
Documented:
. called: "Axiom of Infinity"
. description: "There is a set $I$ that contains the empty set and is closed
                under the successor operation. Such a set cannot be finite, so
                this axiom is what first admits an infinite set into the theory."
Id: "a0f45971-fcc1-419b-bcd5-f4c4717bb37f"
